Q("ml-021","You forecast sales for each store, each region and the whole company, and the numbers don't add up. What do you do?","ML","hard",S,"Senior DS interview, forecasting at scale",
 [("What is the difference between bottom-up and top-down?","test the simple approaches"),
  ("What does reconciliation do?","test optimal combination"),
  ("How do you evaluate the result?","test accuracy at every level")],
 "Forecasts made separately at each level disagree; reconciliation adjusts them so they add up, usually improving accuracy as well as consistency.",
 "I would forecast the stores and add them up to get the region and company totals.",
 "Separate models at each level won't sum. Bottom-up sums store forecasts, which is consistent but noisy for sparse stores. Top-down splits a company forecast by historical proportions, which is smooth but loses store detail. Reconciliation methods take forecasts from every level and adjust them jointly so they add up, weighting each by how reliable it is; this often improves accuracy at all levels because each level contributes information. I would evaluate with walk-forward validation at every level, since the right choice depends on which level decisions are made at.",
 ["forecasting-models","time-series-forecasting"],["forecasting"],"design"),
Q("ml-022","Compare MAPE, RMSE and WAPE for judging a demand forecast. Which would you use?","ML","easy",C,"DS interview, evaluation metrics for forecasting",
 [("What happens to MAPE when actual values are near zero?","test blow-up and asymmetry"),
  ("Why might RMSE and MAE disagree on which model is better?","test sensitivity to large errors"),
  ("How would you match the metric to the business cost?","test cost alignment")],
 "Each metric rewards a different behavior, so choose by what an error costs the business and by whether the data contain small or zero values.",
 "I would use MAPE because percentages are easy to explain.",
 "MAPE is easy to read but explodes when actuals are near zero and penalizes over-forecasts more than under-forecasts, which can push models toward under-forecasting. RMSE squares errors, so it punishes large misses and suits cases where a big miss is disproportionately costly. WAPE divides total absolute error by total actuals, so it is stable and weights high-volume items more, which is often how the business thinks. I would pick the metric from the cost of errors, report more than one, and evaluate at the level where decisions are made.",
 ["forecast-metrics","evaluation-metrics"],["mape"],"concept"),
Q("ml-023","How would you build a recommender for an e-commerce site? Compare matrix factorization with a content-based approach.","ML","medium",C,"DS interview, recommendations",
 [("What happens for a brand-new item or user?","test the cold start problem"),
  ("Explicit ratings are rare. What do you use instead?","test implicit feedback"),
  ("How do you evaluate offline and online?","test ranking metrics and A/B tests")],
 "Collaborative methods learn from behavior but need history; content-based methods work from item attributes but stay narrow. Real systems combine them.",
 "I would use collaborative filtering to recommend what similar users bought.",
 "Matrix factorization learns a vector for each user and item from the interaction matrix so that their product predicts affinity; it captures taste patterns without needing item attributes, but it needs history and handles new items poorly. A content-based approach scores items by similarity to what a user liked using attributes or embeddings; it handles new items but tends to recommend more of the same. Most sites use implicit feedback such as clicks and purchases, and combine the approaches, often with a ranking model on top. I'd evaluate offline with ranking metrics like recall at k or NDCG, with time-based splits, and confirm with an online experiment.",
 ["recommender-systems","feedback-loops"],["matrix-factorization","embedding"],"design"),
Q("ml-024","Which models need feature scaling, and which don't? Why?","ML","easy",C,"Phone screen, ML fundamentals",
 [("What goes wrong if you fit the scaler on all the data?","test leakage"),
  ("Does scaling matter for lasso and ridge?","test penalty sensitivity"),
  ("When would you use standardization versus min-max?","test outliers and bounded ranges")],
 "Scaling matters when a model uses distances or penalizes coefficient size, or when optimization is sensitive to scale; tree models split on order and don't care.",
 "Neural networks and SVMs need scaling, trees don't.",
 "Models based on distances (k-NN, k-means, SVMs), penalized models (ridge, lasso) and gradient-based models like neural networks are sensitive to feature scale: a feature measured in large units dominates distances or gets penalized differently. Tree-based models split on thresholds, so monotonic rescaling changes nothing. I fit the scaler on the training data only and apply it to validation and test data, otherwise information leaks. Standardization is the default; min-max suits bounded inputs but is sensitive to outliers, where a robust scaler helps.",
 ["feature-scaling","data-leakage"],["feature-scaling"],"concept"),
Q("python-015","Implement k-means from scratch with NumPy. What would you check before trusting the result?","Python","medium",C,"Coding screen, ML from scratch",
 [("How would you vectorize the assignment step?","test broadcasting and avoiding loops"),
  ("What do you do with an empty cluster?","test edge cases"),
  ("How do you decide it has converged?","test stopping criteria")],
 "The algorithm is short; the care goes into initialization, empty clusters and stopping, plus running it several times because the result depends on the start.",
 "I would pick random centers, assign points and update the means in a loop.",
 "Initialize k centers by sampling points (ideally k-means++), then repeat: compute distances from every point to every center with broadcasting, assign each point to its nearest center with argmin, and set each center to the mean of its points. If a cluster is empty, re-seed it from a far-away point. Stop when assignments stop changing or the centers move less than a tolerance, with an iteration cap. Run it several times with different seeds and keep the lowest within-cluster sum of squares. Before trusting it I'd check that features are scaled and that the clusters are stable and interpretable.",
 ["python-fundamentals","clustering"],["k-means"],"implementation"),
Q("python-016","Write gradient descent for linear regression using only NumPy. How do you know the learning rate is right?","Python","medium",C,"Coding screen, ML from scratch",
 [("Why does unscaled data make this slow or unstable?","test conditioning and scaling"),
  ("How does this differ from the closed-form solution?","test the normal equation"),
  ("What changes for stochastic or mini-batch updates?","test noisy gradients")],
 "Compute the gradient of the mean squared error, step against it, and watch the loss curve: a good learning rate decreases it smoothly and a bad one diverges or crawls.",
 "I would repeatedly update the weights by subtracting the learning rate times the gradient.",
 "With X as an n by d matrix, predictions are X @ w, the gradient of mean squared error is (2/n) * X.T @ (X @ w - y), and each step is w -= lr * gradient, repeated for a fixed number of iterations or until the loss stops improving. I'd scale features first, because very different scales make the loss surface a narrow valley that forces a tiny learning rate. I'd plot loss per iteration: smooth decline means the rate is fine, oscillation or growth means it is too high, and a very slow decline means it is too low. The closed form is exact for small problems; gradient descent scales to large ones and generalizes to other models. Mini-batches trade noisier steps for much cheaper updates.",
 ["optimization","python-fundamentals"],["gradient-descent","learning-rate"],"implementation"),
Q("python-017","Build a simple document search using TF-IDF and cosine similarity. What are its limits?","Python","medium",C,"Coding screen, text processing",
 [("Why cosine similarity instead of Euclidean distance?","test length normalization"),
  ("How does it handle synonyms?","test lexical matching limits"),
  ("How would it scale to millions of documents?","test sparse matrices and indexes")],
 "TF-IDF search matches words, not meaning: fast and transparent, but blind to synonyms and paraphrase.",
 "I would convert documents to TF-IDF vectors and rank them by cosine similarity to the query.",
 "Fit a TF-IDF vectorizer on the documents to get a sparse matrix, transform the query with the same vectorizer, compute cosine similarity as a sparse dot product (rows are L2-normalized by default), and return the top-scoring documents with argsort. Cosine ignores document length, which Euclidean distance does not. The limits are that it only matches shared words, so synonyms and paraphrases are missed, and that stop words and tokenization choices matter. At large scale I'd use an inverted index such as BM25 in a search engine, or embeddings with an approximate nearest-neighbor index, often combining both.",
 ["text-features","embeddings-and-search","python-fundamentals"],["tf-idf","cosine-similarity"],"implementation"),
Q("llm-014","When would you use LoRA instead of fully fine-tuning a large language model?","LLMs & AI","medium",N,"DS/ML engineer interview, LLM adaptation",
 [("What exactly is trained in LoRA?","test low-rank adapters"),
  ("What are the trade-offs against full fine-tuning?","test capacity and cost"),
  ("Before fine-tuning at all, what would you try?","test prompting and retrieval first")],
 "LoRA freezes the base model and trains small added matrices, giving most of the benefit of fine-tuning at a fraction of the memory and storage.",
 "LoRA is cheaper than full fine-tuning because it trains fewer parameters.",
 "LoRA keeps the pretrained weights frozen and learns small low-rank matrices added to selected layers, so only a tiny share of parameters is trained. That cuts GPU memory, speeds up training and lets you store one small adapter per task and swap them on one base model. Full fine-tuning can capture larger changes in behavior but costs far more and risks forgetting. I'd use LoRA for style, format or domain adaptation with limited data and budget. Before either, I'd check whether prompting or retrieval solves the problem, since fine-tuning is better for behavior than for adding facts.",
 ["prompting-vs-fine-tuning","llm-training"],["lora","pretrained-model"],"concept"),
